## Why This Is Asked
Examines a practical sleep-wake path and basic data-structuring under concurrency.

## Key Concepts
- Timer wheel with fixed slots for constant-time operations
- Safe concurrent enqueue and wakeup via spinlocks
- Tick progression and wrap-around handling

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct Thread { int id; struct Thread *next; } Thread;

#define WHEEL_SIZE 64
typedef struct {
  Thread *buckets[WHEEL_SIZE];
  int current_tick;
  SpinLock lock;
} TimerWheel;

void sleep_ms(Thread *t, int ms, TimerWheel *w) {
  int slot = (w->current_tick + ms) % WHEEL_SIZE;
  spin_lock(&w->lock);
  t->next = w->buckets[slot];
  w->buckets[slot] = t;
  spin_unlock(&w->lock);
}

void timer_tick(TimerWheel *w) {
  spin_lock(&w->lock);
  int slot = w->current_tick % WHEEL_SIZE;
  Thread *cur = w->buckets[slot];
  w->buckets[slot] = NULL;
  w->current_tick++;
  spin_unlock(&w->lock);
  while (cur) { wake(cur); cur = cur->next; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for correctness under concurrent enqueues and wakes?
- What changes if ms can exceed wheel size multiple times?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about live-code updates in a running kernel, including synchronization, cache coherence, and control of instruction fetch paths under contention.

## Key Concepts

- Live patching of executable memory without stopping workers
- Per-page state machines and atomic publish
- I-cache/TLB synchronization and rollback safety

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal C-like sketch (pseudo-code)
enum { STABLE, STAGED, ACTIVE } state_t;
typedef struct { void *addr; void *code; state_t s; volatile int refcnt; uint64_t ver; } page_t;

int publish_patch(page_t *p, void *new_code) {
  p->s = STAGED;
  p->code = new_code;
  __sync_synchronize();
  if (atomic_compare_exchange(&p->s, STAGED, ACTIVE)) {
    atomic_fetch_add(&p->ver, 1);
    icache_invalidate(p->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    tlb_invalidate(p->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    return 0;
  }
  return -1;
}
```

